      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so my lawn is looking kinda yellow and thin in some spots. i saw something about NPK ratios on a bag of fertilizer at the store. can u explain what that actually means and how it works? also whats it look like if my grass is missing phosphorus specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "NPK stands for Nitrogen, Phosphorus, Potassium, which are the three primary nutrients plants need to thrive. These numbers (e.g., 10-10-10) indicate the percentage by weight of each nutrient in the fertilizer. Nitrogen promotes green, leafy growth, while phosphorus is crucial for root and flower development, and potassium supports overall plant health. If your grass is missing phosphorus, you might see stunted root systems, poor early growth, and sometimes purplish discoloration in the leaves.",
